faqTitle: Common questions
faqLink: All 50 answers in the Yelp Hub
faq:
  - q: Should I hire an agency or manage Yelp Ads myself?
    a: >-
      Manage it yourself if your budget is small and you have time to watch it
      weekly. Below roughly a thousand dollars a month in ad spend, the one-time
      setup and optimization fee is hard to justify, the account will not qualify
      for the best partner incentives, and there is not enough click volume to
      tell what is working from what is noise. Above that, or if nobody on staff
      will open the dashboard, a certified partner adds credits, support, and
      tuning you cannot get on your own.
  - q: What do agencies charge to manage Yelp Ads?
    a: >-
      Most charge either a flat monthly fee or a percentage of ad spend, commonly
      10 to 20 percent. We charge neither. There is a one-time setup and
      optimization fee, which is real money and the point at which most of the
      work happens, and after that we are paid by Yelp's rebate on managed spend.
      Some work sits outside that: review management, API and CRM connections,
      and creative production like graphics or video are quoted separately. Ask
      any agency whether they take the rebate as well as a management fee. Most
      partners do.
  # BLANK LINES IN THESE FOLDED SCALARS ARE PARAGRAPH BREAKS AND ARE LOAD-BEARING.
  # A folded scalar keeps a blank line as a newline, and the FAQ body splits on
  # it. Reflowing one of these answers into a single block silently loses the
  # author's paragraphing.
  - q: Do Yelp Ads require a contract?
    a: >-
      Yelp Ads do not require a contract, and on a self-serve account you can
      turn them on or off at any point in the month with a pro-rated bill.

      Managed accounts work differently. We bill centrally, which is how the
      partner pricing and credits are applied in the first place, so we pay Yelp
      and you pay us. That means changes are not instant the way clicking a
      toggle is. It also means pausing mid-month, or mid-promotion, can cost you
      a promotional rate you were partway through earning. Tell us before you
      want something off and we will handle the timing.

      Our management agreement runs on a three-month commitment.
  - q: How does billing work with a certified partner?
    a: >-
      Two ways. Centralized, which is the default, means we are billed by Yelp
      for the ad spend across every account we manage and you are billed by us.
      Decentralized means you pay Yelp directly and we manage the account without
      touching the money.

      Centralized is how the partner program is designed to work. One invoice to
      one agency replaces a hundred separate accounts, and Yelp's sales and
      accounts receivable teams work with a single point of contact instead of
      managing each one individually. That efficiency is part of what the partner
      pricing and the client credits are paid for.

      What it means in practice: your spend runs through us, changes go through
      us rather than through a toggle you click, and the account qualifies for
      pricing and promotions a self-serve advertiser cannot get.

      We can manage a decentralized account in some circumstances, usually where
      a business has a billing structure that will not accommodate it. Ask. But a
      decentralized account sits outside the partner program's pricing, and the
      promotional rates, ad credits, and bundled offers that come with it are not
      available on spend we do not bill.
  - q: Should I buy Yelp Ads from a sales call?
    a: >-
      Not on the call itself. Yelp's sales team works to a quota and the offer will
      still be there tomorrow. Ask for the terms in writing, check the cancellation
      clause, and confirm the budget against what a lead in your category is worth.
      If you already work with a partner, the call routes to them instead.
  - q: Can a certified partner get a bad review removed?
    a: >-
      No. Certification gives a partner an escalation path, not editorial control.
      A review comes down only if it breaks Yelp's content guidelines, and that
      call is Yelp's. Any agency promising review removal is either misreading the
      program or lying about it, and paying for ads does not change what the filter
      does either. What we can do is tell you whether a review is worth flagging
      and on what grounds, and how to respond in public so the next reader sees a
      business that handles problems well. Fifteen years of doing this is mostly
      knowing which fights are worth having.
  - q: Who owns the account if we stop working together?
    a: >-
      You do, in every case. The Yelp account is yours, the ad spend is billed by
      Yelp to you directly, and the profile work stays in place. If we part ways we
      hand back access and the reporting history, and you keep running it or move
      it to someone else.

closing:
  statement: >-
    Send us the business and the market, and we will tell you what a
    partner-managed account would actually do for it.
  body: >-
    Every proposal starts with an evaluation of the account: whether the category,
    the market and the review position can carry paid placement, and whether any
    new-advertiser credit is still available to you. A proposal comes back with a
    recommended budget, the fee, and the numbers we would hold ourselves to. If
    Yelp is not the right place for your budget, we will say so in the same reply.
  cta: Request a proposal
  note: We reply within one business day
